---
title: Privacy
---

This site counts readers. Here is exactly what that means, in plain language.

## Nothing is stored on your device

No cookies. No local storage. No session storage. Nothing this site does
leaves anything behind on your machine, which is why there is no consent
banner to click away: there is nothing to consent to.

## What is recorded

When a page loads, the site records the page you opened, its title, where you
came from if the referring site chose to say, the campaign parameters in the
link if it had any, your screen and window size, your time zone, your
language, whether you prefer dark mode and reduced motion, and a rough
connection type.

## How visitors are counted without identifying anyone

Counting unique readers normally requires tagging the device. Instead, the
server combines your IP address, your browser's user agent and a secret that
changes every day, and keeps only the resulting hash. Your IP address is
discarded immediately and never written down. The daily secret is deleted
after three days, and once it is gone the stored hashes cannot be traced back
to anyone, including by me.

The cost of doing it this way is real: I cannot tell whether you have visited
before, on this device or any other, and I never will. That is the trade, and
I would rather have it this way.

## If you have Global Privacy Control turned on

Nothing is sent at all. Not a reduced record, not an anonymous ping. The
script checks and stops.

## Who sees this

Nobody but me. The data sits on a machine in my home, not with an analytics
company. It is not sold, shared or fed to anything.

The code that does all of this is open source and you are welcome to check
that it does what this page says.
